LAB 6 — PoisonedCredentials | CyberDefenders | Network Forensics | Easy

Scenario: A workstation on the cybercactus.local domain attempted to access a mistyped network share, triggering broadcast name resolution. 
Attacker exploited this fallback to spoof the share and harvest NTLM credentials. 
PCAP analyzed to identify the rogue host and assess credential exposure.

Attack Chain
Victim (192.168.232.162) attempts to resolve fileshaare (mistyped share name) → DNS lookup fails → 
falls back to LLMNR/NBT-NS/MDNS broadcast → Attacker (192.168.232.215) responds to broadcast, spoofing itself as fileshaare → 
Victim authenticates to rogue host via SMB2/NTLM, exposing credentials — MITRE ATT&CK T1557.001 (LLMNR/NBT-NS Poisoning)
Network Indicators

Rogue machine: 192.168.232.215
Victim workstation: 192.168.232.162 (hostname: ACCOUNTING-PC)
Compromised domain: cybercactus.local
Compromised account: janesmith
Protocols abused: LLMNR, NBT-NS, MDNS

Delivery method: broadcast name-resolution spoofing → SMB2 NTLM Session Setup (Negotiate → Challenge → Authenticate)
Attack Technique: LLMNR/NBT-NS Poisoning (Responder-style)
Identified via Wireshark protocol/response analysis
Credential theft technique targeting Windows' legacy broadcast name resolution fallback
No malware payload involved — pure network spoofing/credential interception
Confirms attacker was actively listening on the local segment (adjacent-network position)

Tools Used: Wireshark, display filters (llmnr, mdns, nbns, smb2, ntlmssp.auth.username, ntlmssp.auth.hostname)
Concepts Learned: LLMNR/NBT-NS broadcast fallback mechanics, query-vs-response pivoting for attacker identification, NTLM 3-message handshake (Negotiate/Challenge/Authenticate), NTLMSSP field extraction (username/domain/hostname), credential harvesting via name-resolution spoofing

LAB LOCATION: https://cyberdefenders.org/blueteam-ctf-challenges/poisonedcredentials/
